    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A temple town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s Ancash highlands, c. 950–400 BCE, whose priests drew pilgrims and offerings from the coast, mountains and forests.</li><li>Its stone galleries hid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzon">Lanzón</a>, a carved granite god, and its art of jaguars, eagles and caimans spread far across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</li><li>It held sway through religion rather than armies; after its decline regional cultures such as Paracas developed their own styles.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2001 archaeologists found twenty decorated trumpets made from conch shells, brought from the seas off Ecuador, hidden in a small gallery beside the temple’s Circular Plaza.</p></aside>
    </section>

